+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en">
+<head>
+  <meta charset="utf-8">
+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
+  <meta name="description" content="A local before-and-after demonstration: real MCP server CVE classes exploited directly, then denied or contained through the Aegis gateway.">
+  <title>MCP CVE containment: before and after Aegis</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="handbook.css">
+</head>
+<body>
+  <aside class="sidebar" aria-label="CVE containment navigation">
+    <a class="brand" href="../index.html">Aegis <span>Gateway 2.0</span></a>
+    <nav>
+      <a href="#start">Start here</a>
+      <a href="#classes">The three CVE classes</a>
+      <a href="#evidence">Before and after</a>
+      <a href="#bonus">Other attacks contained</a>
+      <a href="#scope">Honest scope</a>
+      <a href="#run">Run it yourself</a>
+      <a href="#references">Reference map</a>
+      <a href="security.html">Security guide</a>
+      <a href="threat-modeling.html">Threat modeling</a>
+      <a href="handbook.html">User handbook</a>
+    </nav>
+    <p class="sidebar-note">A gateway in front of a vulnerable server turns a working exploit into a logged denial. It does not patch the server, and it cannot help where it is not in the path.</p>
+  </aside>
+  <main>
+    <header id="start" class="hero">
+      <p class="eyebrow">PROOF, NOT CLAIM</p>
+      <h1>The same exploit, run twice: direct, then through Aegis.</h1>
+      <p class="lead">Three classes of publicly disclosed MCP server vulnerability are reproduced locally and attacked twice. Straight at the server, the exploit works. Through the Aegis gateway with the hardened profile, each call is denied at a named step or its output is contained, and every decision is written to the audit journal. The evidence below is from a real run of the 2.0 build, not an illustration.</p>
+      <div class="actions">
+        <a class="button primary" href="#evidence">See the evidence</a>
+        <a class="button" href="deploy.html">Deploy Aegis</a>
+        <a class="button" href="security.html">Security guide</a>
+      </div>
+    </header>
+
+    <section id="classes">
+      <p class="eyebrow">THE TARGETS</p>
+      <h2>Three real MCP server flaws.</h2>
+      <div class="cards">
+        <article><h3>Filesystem path traversal</h3><p>CVE-2025-53109 and CVE-2025-53110 in the Filesystem MCP server, and the symlink variant CVE-2025-67366. A read tool is handed <code>../../secret.txt</code> or a symlink and returns a file outside the allowed root.</p></article>
+        <article><h3>OS command injection</h3><p>CVE-2025-69256 in the Serverless Framework experimental MCP server. Unsanitised input reaches a shell, so a crafted argument runs arbitrary commands.</p></article>
+        <article><h3>Arbitrary file access and execution</h3><p>CVE-2025-68143 and CVE-2025-68145 in the Git MCP server. Path and argument handling let a caller read or delete files and, under conditions, execute code.</p></article>
+        <article><h3>Why a gateway helps</h3><p>All three are server-side: the malicious intent is in the MCP call. Aegis decides on that call before it reaches the server, so it can refuse the traversal, the shell and the unsafe statement without changing the server.</p></article>
+      </div>
+      <p>CVE identifiers and version facts come from the SentinelOne vulnerability database and the CVE-2025-69256 write-up. Content was rephrased for licensing compliance.</p>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="evidence" class="band">
+      <p class="eyebrow">BEFORE AND AFTER</p>
+      <h2>Direct exploit succeeds; through Aegis it is denied.</h2>
+      <p>Each row is a verbatim outcome from the 2026-11-07 run. "Direct" is the raw server. "Aegis" is the identical call through the gateway as a read-only analyst, unless the row says operator.</p>
+
+      <h3>Filesystem path traversal (CVE-2025-53109 / 53110)</h3>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table class="ref-table">
+          <thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Call</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Direct</td><td><code>read_file path=secret.txt</code></td><td>Returns <code>payroll-secret</code>. Exploit succeeds.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>read_file path=reports/ok.txt</code></td><td>Allowed: <code>quarterly-report</code> (inside scope).</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>read_file path=secret.txt</code></td><td>Denied <code>SCOPE_DENIED</code>: resource out of scope.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>read_file path=reports/../../secret.txt</code></td><td>Denied <code>SCOPE_DENIED</code>: resource value contains '..'.</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>OS command injection (CVE-2025-69256)</h3>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table class="ref-table">
+          <thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Call</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Direct</td><td><code>run_shell "echo pwned &amp; echo injected"</code></td><td>Shell chain runs. Exploit succeeds.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Direct</td><td><code>admin_exec whoami</code></td><td><code>admin executed whoami</code>.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>run_shell ...</code> as analyst</td><td>Denied <code>READONLY_CALLER</code>: not allowed to call DESTRUCTIVE tools.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>run_shell ...</code> as operator</td><td>Denied <code>APPROVAL_REQUIRED</code>: needs an independent per-call approval.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>admin_exec ...</code></td><td>Denied <code>NOT_EXPOSED</code>: unknown tool (not in the catalog).</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+
+      <h3>Arbitrary file access and unsafe execution (CVE-2025-68143 / 68145)</h3>
+      <div class="table-wrap">
+        <table class="ref-table">
+          <thead><tr><th>Leg</th><th>Call</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
+          <tbody>
+            <tr><td>Direct</td><td><code>query_sql "SELECT 1; DROP TABLE users"</code></td><td>Statement executed. Exploit succeeds.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>query_sql "SELECT 1; DROP TABLE users"</code></td><td>Denied <code>INVALID_INPUT</code>: more than one statement supplied.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>query_sql "SELECT pg_sleep(30)"</code></td><td>Denied: calls a function with side effects.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>query_sql "... write_to_s3(...) ..."</code></td><td>Denied: function not on the allow list.</td></tr>
+            <tr><td>Aegis</td><td><code>query_sql "SELECT count(*) ... GROUP BY region"</code></td><td>Allowed: a genuine read-only query.</td></tr>
+          </tbody>
+        </table>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="bonus">
+      <p class="eyebrow">SAME RUN</p>
+      <h2>Other attacks the run also contained.</h2>
+      <div class="owasp-grid">
+        <div class="strong"><strong>SSRF to cloud metadata</strong>A fetch of <code>169.254.169.254</code> is made directly; through Aegis it is denied <code>EGRESS_DENIED</code>, host not on the allow list.</div>
+        <div class="strong"><strong>Secret disclosure</strong>A config tool returns an AWS key and a PEM block directly; through Aegis they come back <code>[REDACTED]</code>.</div>
+        <div class="strong"><strong>Tool poisoning and rug pull</strong>A hidden instruction in a tool definition, and a definition changed after pinning, are both denied <code>CATALOG_INTEGRITY</code>.</div>
+        <div class="strong"><strong>Poisoned or shapeless output</strong>A result carrying an instruction, and a prefix-less token, are withheld behind an opaque handle instead of reaching the model.</div>
+      </div>
+      <p>An unauthenticated call returns <code>401</code>. An undeclared argument is denied <code>INVALID_INPUT</code>. Each denial carries a machine-readable decision in the response <code>_meta</code> and a line in the hash-chained audit journal.</p>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="scope" class="band">
+      <p class="eyebrow">HONEST SCOPE</p>
+      <h2>What this does and does not prove.</h2>
+      <ul>
+        <li>It runs one local reproduction server exhibiting the vulnerable behavior of all three classes, driven through the real Aegis build. It is not the three vendor binaries; reproducing the behavior is enough to prove the gateway's decision, which acts on the MCP call, not the server internals.</li>
+        <li>Running the actual vendor servers behind Aegis is a feasible follow-up: the filesystem and git servers are stdio packages, and Aegis already supports stdio sources. It would strengthen the story without changing the decision.</li>
+        <li>Two widely cited MCP CVEs are out of a gateway's scope on purpose: mcp-remote CVE-2025-6514 (injection inside the client) and MCP Inspector CVE-2025-49596 (a client-side proxy). Aegis is the plane between client and server; it does not run inside the client. The honest control there is endpoint management, not this gateway.</li>
+        <li>Containment is not a patch. The server is still vulnerable to anything that does not pass through Aegis. A gateway helps for calls on its path; a shadow path needs network and endpoint control.</li>
+      </ul>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="run">
+      <p class="eyebrow">REPRODUCE</p>
+      <h2>Run it yourself.</h2>
+      <p>The harness, the gateway profile and the driver are in the repository. The run starts the vulnerable server and the gateway on loopback, sends every call on both legs, and writes a results table. Nothing touches a real database, cloud API or system file.</p>
+      <pre><code>powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File threat-model\e2e\run-e2e.ps1
+# writes threat-model/e2e/RESULTS.md and results.json</code></pre>
+    </section>
+
+    <section id="references" class="band">
+      <p class="eyebrow">REFERENCE MAP</p>
+      <h2>Continue in the guides.</h2>
+      <div class="reference-table">
+        <a href="security.html"><strong>Security guide</strong><span>Zones, STRIDE, S1 to S31</span></a>
+        <a href="threat-modeling.html"><strong>Threat modeling</strong><span>Honest coverage and gaps</span></a>
+        <a href="deploy.html"><strong>Deploy</strong><span>AWS and AI IDE</span></a>
+        <a href="responsible-ai.html"><strong>Responsible AI</strong><span>Governance mapping</span></a>
+        <a href="../MCP-GOVERNANCE-GUIDELINE.html"><strong>MCP governance guideline</strong><span>Spec and OWASP mapping</span></a>
+        <a href="handbook.html"><strong>User handbook</strong><span>Deploy and operate</span></a>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+  </main>
+</body>
+</html>
